panvk: the Bifrost compiler's register allocator spills in growing batches, not one value per pass

Applied by tools/mk-panvk.sh only, after tools/mesa/*.patch and the panvk patches before it, to Mesa 25.0.7 (Mesa's own licence, MIT,
applies to the lines it changes). Written for Scion. The OpenGL ES driver (tools/mk-mesa.sh) does not take it.

Why. bi_register_allocate() tries to colour the whole program, and when it fails it spills the one value that interferes most
with the node that failed, then builds the interference graph again from nothing and tries again, up to 2000 times. metal2vulkan's
capture replay kernels (the Astronomy wallpaper's first pass, docs/metal.md, phases 5 and 7) spill about a thousand values, and
each pass over them costs a second or more of the Mali-G72's processor in bi_allocate_registers' liveness and interference
(301 and 73 of 501 samples of a spindump), so the kernel took minutes to make, and AegirPoster's extension sat at 100% of a
core for 9 minutes and more.

What. A shader that colours after a few spills spills exactly as before: the first BI_SPILL_RAMP (8) failed passes each spill the
best one value, as before. A shader that keeps failing spills twice as many values every 8 passes, up to BI_SPILL_BATCH_MAX (64)
at a time, the best ones by the same measure (the most constraints), all among the values that interfere with the node that
failed. A thousand spills then take about 60 passes, not a thousand.


--- a/src/panfrost/compiler/bi_ra.c
